<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Go-Murakami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1328">1328</a>–<a href="/years/1368">1368</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Go-Murakami (1328–1368) was the second emperor of the Southern Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, reigning from 1339 to 1368 after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a>. Based mostly in the mountains of Yoshino and later Anō, he relied on loyalist warrior families such as the Kitabatake and Kusunoki, and his forces briefly retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> several times during the wars with the Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-go-murakami.jpg" alt="Emperor Go-Murakami" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Go-Murakami_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Go-Murakami, whose personal name was Noriyoshi, was a seventh son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a>; his mother was Lady Renshi. He was named after the 10th-century Emperor Murakami, and the prefix go- means later, so he is sometimes called Murakami II. When Go-Daigo began the Kenmu Restoration, the still very young prince went in 1333 with Kitabatake Akiie to Tagajō in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu Province</a> to bring the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> back to their allegiance and destroy the remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>.</p><p>In 1336 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> rose in rebellion, and after Takauji defeated the imperial side at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> the prince returned to Mutsu. In 1337 Tagajō was attacked and he went west again, to Yoshino, fighting constantly. In October 1338 he was named Crown Prince.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He became emperor in September 1339 on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitabatake-chikafusa">Kitabatake Chikafusa</a> as his adviser. He reigned from Sumiyoshi, Yoshino and other temporary locations. In 1348 Kō no Moronao attacked Yoshino and the court moved to Nishiyoshino, and in 1352 the emperor entered Otokoyama in Yamashiro Province.</p><p>In April 1352, after the Battle of Shichijō Ōmiya, Kusunoki Masanori recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> from Ashikaga Yoshiakira, and the retired Northern emperors Kōgon, Kōmyō and Sukō were taken to Anau, where the Southern Court sat. Within twenty days Yoshiakira had retaken the city. The emperor was confined at Otokoyama but escaped to Kawachi Province during an attack and a few months later returned to Yoshino.</p><p>In 1361 Hosokawa Kiyōji and Kusunoki Masanori, who had returned to the Southern Court's allegiance, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and held it briefly, but Yoshiakira quickly responded and they left in under twenty days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Southern Court tried to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> again, but its power was weakening. By the emperor's death on 29 March 1368, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> was in power and the throne had been moved to Sumiyoshi. His sons Yutanari and Hironari later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-chokei">Emperor Chōkei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-kameyama">Emperor Go-Kameyama</a>. His tomb, Hinoo no misasagi, lies in the precincts of Kanshin-ji temple in Kawachinagano, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Go-Murakami” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its emperors after Go-Daigo were <b>Go-Murakami</b>, Chokei and Go-Kameyama.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-court">Southern Court</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Sumiyoshi Angū, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.61,135.49&amp;year=1354" aria-label="Sumiyoshi Angū on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7991 911 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Emperor Go-Murakami"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1181h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1181h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.61,135.49&amp;year=1354">What was here in 1354? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
